export const STROPHEN_EN = {
  "1-die-bruecke": {"t":"The Bridge","b":"It is not the world that grounds the connection – the connection invents the world that afterwards makes it appear necessary.","x":"Every theory begins with a bridge. It links concepts before it even knows whether anything is waiting for it on the other side. Therein lies its real daring. Its first step is not truth but the presumption of a connection. Only when later operations take up this connection again does it acquire the appearance of a necessary order. What we call an origin is therefore usually nothing other than the first successful connection. Bridges are not built because banks exist; banks come into being because bridges have learned to remember their own direction.","r":[{"w":"Soziale Systeme","d":"Sociology","n":"Operations gain coherence only through connection."},{"w":"Meeting the Universe Halfway","d":"Philosophy","n":"Relations do not presuppose relata that are already given."},{"w":"Measures of Distance","d":"Art","n":"Closeness and distance emerge in a mediated relationship."},{"w":"Die Brücke","d":"Literature","n":"The bridge itself becomes the precarious event of connecting."}]},
  "2-das-ereignis": {"t":"The Event","b":"What has happened has happened; its history changes with every connection.","x":"An event takes place and is over the next moment. What has happened cannot afterwards be undone. What remains open, by contrast, is the place it will be given in a history. A later connection can turn it into a beginning, a mistake, a coincidence or a turning point. The idea of a straight line of development reads a direction into earlier moments that was still open at the time. Every present selects traces and orders them anew. In doing so it changes the history that can be told about what happened. Precisely because the event is past, its meaning is never settled once and for all.","r":[{"w":"Time and Social Theory","d":"Sociology","n":"Social time arises in practices of ordering and expectation."},{"w":"Process and Reality","d":"Philosophy","n":"Reality is conceived as a happening of events and processes."},{"w":"Today Series","d":"Art","n":"The date fixes an event without determining its later meaning."},{"w":"Mrs Dalloway","d":"Literature","n":"A single day is continually layered anew through memory and present."}]},
  "3-der-anschluss": {"t":"The Connection","b":"Continuity is not a state – it is the most improbable of all operations.","x":"The real achievement of a system lies not in producing something but in carrying on. Every operation vanishes in the moment it is performed. Duration therefore arises not from persistence but from resumption. What we call identity is nothing other than the successful habit of connecting the next step to the previous one. Every failed connection, by contrast, opens up a different history. Systems therefore live not on stability but on the improbable ability to turn their own breaking off into continuation, again and again.","r":[{"w":"Soziale Systeme","d":"Sociology","n":"Autopoiesis consists in the recursive production of further operations."},{"w":"La différance","d":"Philosophy","n":"Continuation rests on trace, repetition and displacement."},{"w":"Bichos","d":"Art","n":"Form arises anew each time in the performance of its next articulation."},{"w":"A paixão segundo G.H.","d":"Literature","n":"Experience continues as its previous order falls apart."}]},
  "4-der-beobachter": {"t":"The Observer","b":"The observer can observe the distinction of their present observing only later.","x":"To observe means to use a distinction and to indicate one of its sides. The observer does not stand over against this operation as an additional authority: the observer becomes determinable only through the way of observing. While seeing something, the observer cannot at the same time observe, as the same operation, the distinction with which they are seeing. A later observation can make this precondition visible; to do so it again uses a distinction of its own. Self-observation thus shifts its blind spot instead of abolishing it once and for all. Even the supposed beginning of observing appears as a beginning only from a later perspective.","r":[{"w":"On Constructing a Reality","d":"Cybernetics","n":"An observer cannot be subtracted from their own construction of reality; every later observation again brings its own preconditions."},{"w":"Situated Knowledges","d":"Philosophy","n":"Every observation has a situated standpoint and therefore limits."},{"w":"Untitled Film Stills","d":"Art","n":"Observation and observed identity displace one another."},{"w":"Pierre Menard, Autor des Quijote","d":"Literature","n":"The same sign becomes a different one through the place from which it is observed."}]},
  "5-der-blinde-fleck": {"t":"The Blind Spot","b":"The blind spot is not the end of knowledge – it is its beginning.","x":"Every operation takes place under conditions it cannot itself observe. Not because it lacks information, but because its own form determines what may appear as information at all. The blind spot is therefore not a deficit of knowledge but its precondition. Whoever wanted to remove it would have to step outside their own operation – and in doing so would produce a new distinction with a new blind spot. Knowledge widens its field of vision not by overcoming blindness, but by learning to vary its own limits productively.","r":[{"w":"Die Beobachtung der Kybernetik","d":"Sociology","n":"Second-order observation makes the selectivity of observing visible."},{"w":"Laws of Form","d":"Philosophy","n":"Every indication presupposes an unmarked side."},{"w":"La condition humaine","d":"Art","n":"Image and world are distinguished by an invisible boundary."},{"w":"A paixão segundo G.H.","d":"Literature","n":"Knowledge begins where familiar distinctions become untenable."}]},
  "6-die-verspaetung": {"t":"The Delay","b":"The present is the art of overlooking its own delay.","x":"Every present appears to itself as immediate. In fact it always arrives too late. Even before an operation experiences itself as present, it has already passed and can only be taken up again through further operations. What we call presence is therefore not a point in time but an ongoing reconstruction. The present lives on continually forgetting its own delay. It is precisely this small lag that makes memory, expectation and connection possible. Without it there would be neither time nor history, only the silent extinguishing of isolated events.","r":[{"w":"Time and Social Theory","d":"Sociology","n":"The present is socially organised and never merely a neutral point in time."},{"w":"Vorlesungen zur Phänomenologie des inneren Zeitbewusstseins","d":"Philosophy","n":"Retention and protention structure the lived now."},{"w":"In Free Fall","d":"Art","n":"The present appears as an unstable state of continued displacement."},{"w":"À la recherche du temps perdu","d":"Literature","n":"The past becomes effective only in later presents."}]},
  "7-die-zeit": {"t":"Time","b":"Time is the condition that something other than its repetition can follow an operation.","x":"Time does not carry events like a current from one place to another. An operation ends with its performance; what has happened cannot be repeated as if it were still present. At the same time a later operation can connect to it and, under changed conditions, make something else of it. Here lies the tension between past and future: what has happened is fixed, its possible continuations are not. Duration arises when connections become possible again; a break changes their conditions. In this stanza, time names the difference that every resumption carries with it: what comes next can connect without going on in the same way.","r":[{"w":"Soziale Systeme; Die Zukunft kann nicht beginnen","d":"Sociology","n":"Time orders actuality and non-actuality within present operations."},{"w":"Thinking with Whitehead","d":"Philosophy","n":"Temporality is thought from becoming and irreversible relations."},{"w":"The Clock","d":"Art","n":"Chronological time is assembled from thousands of heterogeneous film presents."},{"w":"To the Lighthouse","d":"Literature","n":"Duration arises in the displacement between event, memory and absence."}]},
  "8-der-speicher": {"t":"The Store","b":"Storage keeps something available; resumption gives it a new present.","x":"A library keeps books, a hard drive data. Such stores keep material states available and protect them against rapid disappearance. That does not yet explain why something gains meaning years later. A recovered book has to be read, a data set selected, a find brought into a question. Storage creates a possible later encounter; remembering begins with its present performance. The two often need each other, but they do not coincide. Even a fully preserved document can become illegible if its language and its context are lost. The store secures availability; it does not decide the next use.","r":[{"w":"Soziales Vergessen","d":"Sociology","n":"Memory is an achievement of selection and not mere filing."},{"w":"Matière et mémoire","d":"Philosophy","n":"Memory cannot be reduced to material storage."},{"w":"Réserve","d":"Art","n":"Keeping produces a present of absence."},{"w":"Always Coming Home","d":"Literature","n":"Archives are staged as later constructions of cultural reuse."}]},
  "9-das-gedaechtnis": {"t":"Memory","b":"Memory makes the past effective again in changed practices.","x":"Memory shows itself when the past becomes effective again in a present practice. A story calls up earlier events differently from a gesture, a practised movement of the hand or a ritual. Such resumptions have a history; they take place under conditions that have changed. The body, too, carries habits and abilities without having an image of the past ready at every moment. Memory can therefore be reduced neither to filing nor to conscious recollection. It is bound to materials, bodies and social forms and remains at the same time a present achievement. What returns, returns in a new situation.","r":[{"w":"How Societies Remember","d":"Sociology","n":"Bodily practices and rituals pass on social memory without it being reducible to conscious representation."},{"w":"Différence et répétition","d":"Philosophy","n":"Repetition produces difference instead of identical return."},{"w":"Atrabiliarios","d":"Art","n":"Traces of the past remain effective without becoming present."},{"w":"Beloved","d":"Literature","n":"The past returns to the present transformed and capable of acting."}]},
  "10-die-erinnerung": {"t":"Remembering","b":"Remembering gives what has happened a present, verifiable shape.","x":"Remembering happens in the present. When an experience is told, an image looked at or an earlier sentence read again, the past takes on a present-day shape. This shape can change without the past event itself becoming a different one after the fact. Memories draw on traces, fill in gaps and are bound to expectations. They can contradict one another; objects and testimonies at the same time set limits to their interpretations. What is remembered is therefore neither an unchanged copy nor a free invention. Remembering establishes a relation to what has happened and can only be questioned anew from later presents.","r":[{"w":"Les cadres sociaux de la mémoire","d":"Sociology","n":"Memory is reconstructed within social frameworks."},{"w":"Between Past and Future","d":"Philosophy","n":"The past gains meaning from the in-between position of the present."},{"w":"Douleur exquise","d":"Art","n":"Repeated telling transforms the remembered experience."},{"w":"À la recherche du temps perdu","d":"Literature","n":"Remembering produces a new present of the past."}]},
  "11-die-spur": {"t":"The Trace","b":"The trace holds a past effect materially without fixing its meaning.","x":"A footprint in the sand, a yellowed photograph or a crease in paper remembers nothing. Yet every trace has a material constitution that makes later interpretations possible and limits them. The print does not by itself say who passed by; nor can any movement whatsoever be attributed to it. Whether a trace becomes evidence, a story, a work of art or rubbish is decided in a later practice. Its material remains stubborn in the process. The trace links a past occurrence with possible present questions without already carrying the answer within itself.","r":[{"w":"Erinnerungsräume","d":"Sociology","n":"Material traces need cultural forms of reactivation."},{"w":"De la grammatologie","d":"Philosophy","n":"The trace undermines the idea of pure presence."},{"w":"House","d":"Art","n":"Absence is shaped as a materially legible trace."},{"w":"Austerlitz","d":"Literature","n":"Images and places become precarious triggers of late memory."}]},
  "12-das-vergessen": {"t":"Forgetting","b":"Forgetting changes what remains within reach, and for whom.","x":"Forgetting takes several forms. A document can be destroyed, an ability can wither, a name can disappear from public records. In other cases a trace remains, but the paths to it become unusable or improbable. Social forgetting thus helps to order what can be told, examined or expected again. This selection brings relief; but it can also push people and events out of reachable memory. Forgetting is therefore neither merely a lack nor always a creative achievement. Its consequences depend on what is lost, for whom something becomes inaccessible and who can decide about it.","r":[{"w":"Soziales Vergessen","d":"Sociology","n":"Forgetting is a positive condition of selective remembering."},{"w":"Zur Genealogie der Moral","d":"Philosophy","n":"Active forgetting creates room for the present and for action."},{"w":"Shibboleth","d":"Art","n":"The closed trace remains visible as a difference that has not been removed."},{"w":"Funes el memorioso","d":"Literature","n":"Total recall appears as an inability to abstract."}]},
  "13-das-archiv": {"t":"The Archive","b":"The archive keeps and orders what can be found again in the future.","x":"An archive keeps documents and objects. At the same time it orders them according to rules that determine what can be taken in, described and found again. These rules create future possibilities and limit them: without cataloguing, even what has been preserved remains hard to reach; what was never collected cannot simply be called back. A file becomes evidence only through questions, procedures and contradiction. Archives therefore do not tell history by themselves. They provide material and shape which histories can be made from it. Their achievement lies in combining preservation, selection and access – and thus also in the power to set these conditions.","r":[{"w":"Erinnerungsräume","d":"Sociology","n":"Archives organise cultural availability beyond present memory."},{"w":"L’archéologie du savoir","d":"Philosophy","n":"His concept of the archive refers to rules of what can be said and repeated; it widens and unsettles the institutional concept of the archive discussed here."},{"w":"The Atlas Group","d":"Art","n":"The form of the archive and the status of the document are destabilised artistically."},{"w":"Nox","d":"Literature","n":"The private archive remains fragmentary and produces meaning only in its arrangement."}]},
  "14-die-geschichte": {"t":"History","b":"History interprets the past anew and remains bound to its traces.","x":"Past events have happened, even if no one tells of them any longer. History arises when a society orders, disputes and reinterprets its traces, testimonies and traditions. With every present, other questions and previously overlooked documents can become important. That changes the history that can be told; what has happened does not thereby become arbitrary. A claim must prove itself against the available traces, against other accounts and against their gaps. History is neither identical with the archive nor the mere sum of memories. It is a contested, present work on a past that is not exhausted by its use today.","r":[{"w":"La mémoire collective","d":"Sociology","n":"The past is reconstructed in changing social frameworks."},{"w":"Between Past and Future","d":"Philosophy","n":"History stands in a present between origin and possibility."},{"w":"The J. Street Project","d":"Art","n":"Documentation changes what remains visible as rememberable history."},{"w":"Die Ringe des Saturn","d":"Literature","n":"History arises from montage, trace and present wandering."}]},
  "15-die-adresse": {"t":"The Address","b":"The address makes social attribution possible across absence.","x":"Communication needs addresses to which statements, expectations and answers can be attributed. A name, an organisation or a technical account makes someone or something reachable even when its bearer is absent. Such addresses come into being and change through repeated reference. They are not, however, the origin of every bodily or mental occurrence. Their domain is social attribution: who is addressed as the same place, who can answer, who remains responsible? An address keeps these possibilities open across individual situations. How someone is addressed influences their social identity without coinciding with their life.","r":[{"w":"Die Form „Person“","d":"Sociology","n":"Communication bundles expectations onto recognisable social addresses."},{"w":"Relating Narratives","d":"Philosophy","n":"The who of a person emerges relationally and narratively."},{"w":"Suite vénitienne","d":"Art","n":"A person appears through addressable traces and observations."},{"w":"Le città invisibili","d":"Literature","n":"Names hold together places whose identity changes in the telling."}]},
  "16-der-name": {"t":"The Name","b":"The name makes absence addressable.","x":"A name does not say who someone is. It ensures that the same address can be called up again even when its bearer has become absent, changed or contradictory. Names condense past addressings into a form to which expectations, stories and responsibilities connect. Therein lie their power and their violence. Whoever receives a name becomes socially findable, but also fixed; whoever is renamed loses not just a word but an already organised future of possible attributions. The name therefore belongs entirely to no one. It circulates between one's own use and the use of others and holds a social unity together where no identity is sufficient unto itself.","r":[{"w":"Language and Symbolic Power","d":"Sociology","n":"Naming works as social institution and attribution."},{"w":"Relating Narratives","d":"Philosophy","n":"The proper name keeps open the uniqueness of an addressable person."},{"w":"Naming the Money","d":"Art","n":"Naming gives anonymised figures back their history and social address."},{"w":"City of Glass","d":"Literature","n":"Names, roles and identities are drawn into a game of mutual substitution."}]},
  "17-die-person": {"t":"The Person","b":"The person is the memory of an address.","x":"A person is not the human being it designates. It is the form in which communication bundles numerous expectations onto a recognisable address. Whoever is considered friendly, unreliable, ill, responsible or dangerous is not fully described but prepared for further connections. Therein lies the relief the person provides: no one has to be invented anew in every encounter. Therein lies at the same time its harshness: what was once attributed returns as expectation and limits what can still appear credible in future. Persons therefore consist neither of properties nor of inwardness. They come into being as addressings condense and expect their own repetition.","r":[{"w":"Inklusion, Exklusion und die Person","d":"Sociology","n":"Persons function as social forms of attribution."},{"w":"Giving an Account of Oneself","d":"Philosophy","n":"Self-description remains bound to social norms and addressings."},{"w":"Les Suisses morts","d":"Art","n":"Persons become socially present through names, images and archived traces."},{"w":"Der Prozess","d":"Literature","n":"A person is determined by attributions whose order they do not control."}]},
  "18-die-rolle": {"t":"The Role","b":"The role makes expectations independent of persons.","x":"A role is not a mask behind which the real person hides. It is an order of expectations that already exists before anyone takes it on. Teacher, mother, patient, artist or judge do not designate properties but positions from which certain actions become probable and others call for explanation. Roles relieve persons because they need not bring their whole individuality into every situation. At the same time they set limits: whoever takes on a role is measured against expectations that did not come into being with them. In this way the role makes social relationships repeatable by making persons interchangeable without replacing them entirely.","r":[{"w":"The Presentation of Self in Everyday Life","d":"Sociology","n":"Roles structure situations, expectations and self-presentations."},{"w":"Gender Trouble","d":"Philosophy","n":"Repeated performance stabilises social identities without a fixed core."},{"w":"Album","d":"Art","n":"Role, face and biographical attribution are shifted against one another."},{"w":"Sei personaggi in cerca d’autore","d":"Literature","n":"Character, role and person come apart from one another."}]},
  "19-die-erwartung": {"t":"Expectation","b":"Expectations order what can come as a surprise at an address.","x":"Expectations do not predict with certainty what will happen. They determine what can count as fitting, deviant or surprising. A name, a role and a familiar relationship bring such expectations with them; through them the future is already ordered in the present. Disappointments can change an expectation. But they can also lead to its reinforcement when the deviation is treated as an exception or attributed to a person. Precisely therein lies its social persistence. An expectation links an address with possible next events and at the same time determines how the unexpected can be dealt with.","r":[{"w":"Die Fiktion der wahrscheinlichen Realität","d":"Sociology","n":"Expectations structure uncertainty without fixing the future."},{"w":"Vergangene Zukunft","d":"Philosophy","n":"Space of experience and horizon of expectation order historical time."},{"w":"Grapefruit","d":"Art","n":"Instructions produce expectations whose execution remains open."},{"w":"En attendant Godot","d":"Literature","n":"Expectation organises the present without being able to guarantee its fulfilment."}]},
  "20-die-verantwortung": {"t":"Responsibility","b":"Responsibility binds consequences to an address.","x":"Responsibility does not arise inside a person. It arises when communication attributes an action and its consequences to an address. Whoever is held responsible remains reachable for something that has already happened and yet demands further answers. In this, responsibility links past and future: it holds an address fast across changing situations and expects justification, reparation or change from it. This bond can make action possible, but it can also distribute guilt, shift competences and conceal power. Responsibility therefore does not designate a moral property. It is the social procedure through which consequences receive a place capable of answering.","r":[{"w":"Audit Cultures","d":"Sociology","n":"Attribution and accountability organise institutional responsibility."},{"w":"Autrement qu’être","d":"Philosophy","n":"Responsibility begins in being addressable by the Other."},{"w":"¿De qué otra cosa podríamos hablar?","d":"Art","n":"The consequences of social violence are addressed to perception and the public."},{"w":"Disgrace","d":"Literature","n":"Responsibility remains contested between guilt, response and social attribution."}]},
  "21-die-anerkennung": {"t":"Recognition","b":"Recognition gives an address social validity.","x":"Recognition does not discover an identity that already exists. It confirms an address as a valid place of further communication. Whoever is recognised may speak, act, remember and make claims without having to prove their belonging anew in every encounter. Yet recognition is never complete. It grasps a person only in forms that are socially legible, and precisely thereby can overlook or exclude other things about them. Misrecognition, too, keeps addresses effective by attributing alien properties to them. Recognition is therefore not a possession but an ongoing social performance in which it is decided who can appear as whom.","r":[{"w":"The Dignity of Working Men","d":"Sociology","n":"Recognition depends on social boundary-drawing and orders of evaluation."},{"w":"Kampf um Anerkennung","d":"Philosophy","n":"Recognition is understood as a condition of social self-relation."},{"w":"Mastry","d":"Art","n":"Visibility and art-historical recognition are redistributed."},{"w":"Girl, Woman, Other","d":"Literature","n":"Identities are narrated through overlapping relations of recognition and misrecognition."}]},
  "22-die-grenze": {"t":"The Boundary","b":"Only what remains separate can touch.","x":"A boundary does not lie between systems that are already complete. It comes into being with every operation that continues something and excludes something else. Only this distinction produces an inside to which further operations can be attributed, and an outside that does not disappear but remains effective as environment. Boundaries therefore do not prevent relationship; they make it possible. Only what does not dissolve into one another can touch, irritate or couple. The boundary is not a wall but a continually renewed form at which one's own and the other come into being at the same time. Where it falls, what begins is not unity but the loss of any distinguishable relationship.","r":[{"w":"Ecologies of Knowledge","d":"Sociology","n":"Boundary objects coordinate different social worlds without merging them."},{"w":"Brücke und Tür","d":"Philosophy","n":"Boundaries and connections are thought as mutually produced forms."},{"w":"Homebound","d":"Art","n":"Closeness is produced at once by barrier, current and inaccessibility."},{"w":"Vor dem Gesetz","d":"Literature","n":"The boundary exists as a relational order of access and deferral."}]},
  "23-die-beruehrung": {"t":"Touch","b":"Touch is the boundary in the form of an event.","x":"Touch does not abolish a boundary. It is the moment in which two separate orders produce consequences in each other. One hand does not feel the other as the other feels itself; each side produces its own event from the contact. Precisely for this reason touch can be neither possession nor fusion. It changes without transferring anything unchanged. In it the boundary becomes sensitive: pressure, warmth, resistance and closeness appear only because bodies do not become one another. Social touch, too, follows this logic. It lets the foreign become effective without turning it into one's own. Where touch succeeds, difference is preserved not despite closeness but through it.","r":[{"w":"Cold Intimacies","d":"Sociology","n":"Closeness is socially shaped and bound to orders of communication."},{"w":"Corpus","d":"Philosophy","n":"Corporeality appears as being-with at boundaries and surfaces."},{"w":"Bichos","d":"Art","n":"Touch changes the form without uniting work and viewer."},{"w":"On Earth We’re Briefly Gorgeous","d":"Literature","n":"Bodily closeness remains shot through with difference, memory and vulnerability."}]},
  "24-die-irritation": {"t":"Irritation","b":"Irritation is an outside effect in a form of one's own.","x":"An irritation does not penetrate a system from outside. It arises when a foreign change triggers consequences within the system's own distinctions. The same sound can startle, soothe or go unnoticed; it is not the stimulus that determines the reaction but the order that processes it as a deviation. Irritation therefore proves neither openness nor closure alone. It designates the paradoxical point at which a system depends on something it cannot take over. The foreign becomes effective by losing its foreignness and being continued as a difference of the system's own. Coupling begins where such irritations become expectable again.","r":[{"w":"Die Gesellschaft der Gesellschaft","d":"Sociology","n":"Irritation is a system's own processing of differences relating to its environment."},{"w":"Thinking with Whitehead","d":"Philosophy","n":"Relations are thought through mutual conditions of becoming."},{"w":"Blue, Red and Yellow","d":"Art","n":"Perception is irritated by an environment that grants no stable orientation."},{"w":"The Unnamable","d":"Literature","n":"Disturbance becomes the form of a speech that cannot secure its own connection."}]},
  "25-der-koerper": {"t":"The Body","b":"The body turns outside effects into sensitivity.","x":"The body is not a bridge across which the world reaches consciousness unchanged. It is a membrane that lets nothing become effective without transforming it. Light, pressure, temperature or someone else's gaze are not taken over but translated into sensations, movements, tensions and readinesses. Consciousness, too, does not have the body at its disposal as if it were its possession. It encounters it as pain, pleasure, exhaustion or restlessness – as a closeness that never becomes fully transparent. Communication can name, observe and address these states, but it can take over neither the pain nor the perception of another. In the body, the environment becomes sensitive without ceasing to be environment. It couples by separating, and connects by forcing every effect into a form of its own.","r":[{"w":"The Body Multiple","d":"Sociology","n":"Bodies emerge in different practices as distinct, coupled realities."},{"w":"Phénoménologie de la perception","d":"Philosophy","n":"Embodiment is the condition of a pre-reflective relation to the world."},{"w":"Silueta Series","d":"Art","n":"Body and surroundings inscribe themselves into each other without becoming identical."},{"w":"Die Verwandlung","d":"Literature","n":"The body radically changes a person's social connections."}]},
  "26-die-wahrnehmung": {"t":"Perception","b":"Perception makes the boundary between body and consciousness questionable.","x":"Perception makes a theoretical boundary palpable. For Luhmann it is a mental operation: consciousness sees and hears even when it does not reflect on it. The body enables and irritates this performance but cannot simply be equated with consciousness. From Merleau-Ponty's phenomenology another question can be asked: can the lived body be set over against perception as environment at all, if it already performs a pre-reflective relation to the world? This juxtaposition is constructed by the stanza; it is not a direct exchange between the two authors. Precisely their difference shows how much depends on where the boundary between body and consciousness is drawn.","r":[{"w":"Soziale Systeme; Die Kunst der Gesellschaft","d":"Sociology","n":"Perception remains a mental operation and can only irritate communication."},{"w":"Phénoménologie de la perception","d":"Philosophy","n":"Perception is a bodily relation to the world prior to reflective objectification."},{"w":"immersive light and fog works","d":"Art","n":"The conditions of perception themselves become the object of experience."},{"w":"Bieguni / Unrast","d":"Literature","n":"Body, movement and perception appear as unstable orders of closeness."}]},
  "27-die-uebersetzung": {"t":"Translation","b":"Translation is repetition without identity.","x":"Translation transfers nothing unchanged from one order into another. Perception does not become language, pain does not become a message, and a thought does not become communication. Each side produces a form of its own from what reaches it. Therein lies the loss in every translation – and at the same time its possibility: only because sameness is unattainable can something be continued under changed conditions. Here translation touches on Deleuze's concept of repetition, which reproduces nothing identical but brings forth difference. Unlike in Deleuze, however, the concern here is not primarily the creative unfolding of the new, but connection between operationally separate orders. Translation does not remove difference; it makes it connectable.","r":[{"w":"Boundary Objects","d":"Sociology","n":"Translation enables cooperation without complete consensus."},{"w":"Différence et répétition","d":"Philosophy","n":"Repetition produces difference instead of an identical copy."},{"w":"works on language and borders","d":"Art","n":"Transfer shows the political and medial conditions of speaking."},{"w":"Die Aufgabe des Übersetzers","d":"Literature","n":"Translation is afterlife and transformation, not mere copy."}]},
  "28-die-koppelung": {"t":"Coupling","b":"Coupling stabilises effects at boundaries whose emergence itself remains questionable.","x":"Coupling designates a relationship in which distinguishable processes repeatedly become effective for one another. A body does not bring organism, nervous system, consciousness and communication together into a single operation. At it, transitions arise at which each side forms events of its own from effects. Repetition makes such irritations expectable without abolishing differences. The concept thereby explains how separate orders can depend on one another. A further question remains open: how are the boundaries drawn at which we distinguish these orders in the first place? It cannot be answered by appealing to coupling once more, and it makes the relationship itself the object of observation.","r":[{"w":"Soziale Systeme","d":"Sociology","n":"Structural coupling links operational closure with dependence on the environment."},{"w":"Meeting the Universe Halfway","d":"Philosophy","n":"Intra-actions raise the question of how the boundaries and units arise that are distinguished here as orders capable of coupling."},{"w":"After ALife Ahead","d":"Art","n":"Biological, technical and spatial processes remain distinct and influence one another."},{"w":"Emergency","d":"Literature","n":"Human and more-than-human processes are narrated as entangled, non-identical realities."}]},
  "29-das-material": {"t":"The Material","b":"Material is the resistance through which form becomes possible.","x":"Material is not the mute stuff to which an artistic idea gives form after the fact. Wood splinters, paint runs, fabric tightens, metal fatigues, digital systems produce errors and dependencies. In the work, material answers by enabling, deflecting or rejecting intentions. Its resistances are not external obstacles but conditions for something to emerge that was not fully designed beforehand. Even after it has been worked, material remains active: it ages, changes its surface and reacts to light, moisture, touch and time. Art begins where this movement of its own is not eliminated but admitted as participation.","r":[{"w":"The Body Multiple","d":"Sociology","n":"Materiality appears as something that takes part in practices and produces differences."},{"w":"Vibrant Matter","d":"Philosophy","n":"Matter is thought as an effective, not merely passive, condition."},{"w":"Verb List","d":"Art","n":"Material actions and resistances become generators of form."},{"w":"Le Parti pris des choses","d":"Literature","n":"Things resist being simply disposed of by language."}]},
  "30-die-form": {"t":"The Form","b":"Form makes difference reusable.","x":"Form is not a casing forced onto a material after the fact. It is a provisional order in which material, space, time, attention and expectation are related to one another so that a difference can recur. A form does not remain the same because it is unchanged. It remains recognisable although its execution, its context and its meaning change. It is precisely this portability that makes it socially effective: forms can be quoted, shifted, forgotten and taken up again. Where material produces resistance, form makes this resistance distinguishable and available for further operations. It stores nothing; it organises reusability.","r":[{"w":"Forms","d":"Sociology","n":"Forms organise relations and can circulate between situations."},{"w":"Différence et répétition","d":"Philosophy","n":"Form gains recognisability through difference in repetition."},{"w":"Bird in Space","d":"Art","n":"Variation keeps a form recognisable across different material executions."},{"w":"Bluets","d":"Literature","n":"A fragmentary form organises recurrence without linear unity."}]},
  "31-das-werk": {"t":"The Work","b":"A work remains recognisable as a particular form; an artistic address can carry several works.","x":"Not every form is a work. A work needs conditions under which, when executed differently or viewed later, it can be recognised as this work: a title, for instance, a shape, a rule of performance or a documented action. This is to be distinguished from the address of an artist, to which several works can be attributed. Bazon Brock's “artist without a work” shifts precisely the relationship between these two addresses. The later knowledge of Duchamp's Étant donnés changes the story about his public pause from making works, without turning his entire output into a single work. The identity of a work arises through the repeatable attribution of a particular form.","r":[{"w":"Social Works","d":"Sociology","n":"Artworks depend on social infrastructures and forms of support."},{"w":"Ästhetik der Installation","d":"Philosophy","n":"The identity of a work arises in an open relation of object, space and viewer."},{"w":"Künstler ohne Werk / Action Teaching","d":"Art","n":"The status of a work can be organised as an addressed practice rather than as an object."},{"w":"Bartleby y compañía","d":"Literature","n":"Not writing itself becomes observable as a form that can be addressed as literature."}]},
  "32-der-stil": {"t":"Style","b":"Style is the recurrence of a difference as expectation.","x":"Style is not the handwriting in which an unmistakable interior betrays itself. The work binds form to an address; style arises when, at this address, particular ways of choosing, linking and leaving out become recognisable across different works. A rhythm, a blank, a tension of colour or even a refusal can recur without repeating itself. That is why style can change media, outlive the artist and detach itself from them – as a school, an epoch or a manner. Precisely therein lies its danger: what first produced difference congeals into expectation and finally demands of the new that it look once more like its earlier deviation.","r":[{"w":"Façons de lire, manières d’être","d":"Sociology","n":"Style is understood as a form of living, reading and recognising."},{"w":"Ways of Worldmaking","d":"Philosophy","n":"Style functions as a symptomatic form of classification and reference."},{"w":"Kulturgeschichte 1880–1983","d":"Art","n":"Serial procedures, dated materials and historical images link repetition with a recognisable artistic order."},{"w":"10:04","d":"Literature","n":"Style arises in taking up and shifting one's own forms."}]},
  "33-das-publikum": {"t":"The Audience","b":"The audience cultivates the sensitivity of separate perceptions.","x":"An audience is not a crowd that sees the same thing. Perception remains untransferable; no one can take over another person's colour, tension or boredom. Commonality therefore arises not in experience but in what connects to it. People compare, tell, contradict, come back, and in doing so learn to become sensitive to other things. The audience does not unite perceptions. It cultivates conditions under which separate perceptions can irritate and change one another. Therein lies its peculiar sociality: one does not have to have seen the same thing in order to go on seeing the same work differently. The public begins where difference is not removed but reused as a sensitivity that can change.","r":[{"w":"The Passion for Music / Soziologie des amateurs","d":"Sociology","n":"Taste and sensitivity arise in practices of engaging with something."},{"w":"Ästhetik der Installation","d":"Philosophy","n":"Aesthetic experience remains bound to the particular situation of its performance."},{"w":"Caminhando","d":"Art","n":"Cutting a Möbius strip shifts the work into a single, unrepeatable act by those taking part."},{"w":"Palomar","d":"Literature","n":"Observation appears as a sensitivity that can be trained and yet never completed."}]},
  "34-die-ausstellung": {"t":"The Exhibition","b":"The exhibition orders relations before it orders meanings.","x":"An exhibition does not simply show works. It sets them in relation. Closeness, distance, sequence, light, architecture, labelling and routing help determine what becomes visible at the same time and what conceals what. The same work can ask a different question in a different neighbourhood. The exhibition therefore produces no shared perception, but it distributes possibilities of seeing: it accelerates, delays, isolates, condenses and lets transitions emerge. Its order remains provisional; with every step the relation between things changes again. An exhibition is therefore less a container than a choreography – a spatial form of coupling in which works, bodies and expectations gain new sensitivities for one another.","r":[{"w":"The Birth of the Museum","d":"Sociology","n":"Exhibition spaces organise the public, movement and visibility."},{"w":"Double Exposures","d":"Philosophy","n":"Exhibiting is read as a discursive and spatial practice of showing."},{"w":"Mining the Museum","d":"Art","n":"Rearranging the presentation changes the legibility of existing objects."},{"w":"Funny Weather","d":"Literature","n":"The perception of art is described from situations, encounters and the public."}]},
  "35-die-kunst": {"t":"Art","b":"Art keeps perception socially reusable by keeping its difference open.","x":"Art is neither material, form nor work, neither style, audience nor exhibition. It arises when these differences act back on one another in such a way that perception can be taken up socially without being exhausted in an unambiguous message. Material resists, form distinguishes, the work binds to an address, style produces expectation, the audience cultivates sensitivity, the exhibition orders relations. None of these operations possesses art on its own. Only their recursive coupling keeps open a domain in which something can be repeated without remaining the same. Art therefore preserves no meaning. It organises the possibility that perception returns differently.","r":[{"w":"Le paradigme de l’art contemporain","d":"Sociology","n":"Orders of art stabilise themselves through institutions, categories and shifts of boundaries."},{"w":"Aisthesis","d":"Philosophy","n":"Aesthetics orders forms of the perceptible and of taking part in it."},{"w":"Duty Free Art","d":"Art","n":"Images circulate through heterogeneous institutional, technical and economic orders."},{"w":"La carte et le territoire","d":"Literature","n":"Work, the figure of the artist, market and representation are recursively related to one another."}]},
  "36-das-profil": {"t":"The Profile","b":"The profile turns addressable traces into calculable recognisability.","x":"An address makes reachable; a profile makes recognisable. It collects past clicks, purchases, routes, contacts or dwell times and assigns them to an address. What emerges is not an image of the whole person but a selection of comparable features. What appears as a pattern can be compared with other patterns and used for future decisions. Depending on the purpose, the same person is broken down into different calculable segments. Memory thereby shifts towards prediction: the past is used again to make next connections more probable. The addressed human being and the calculated profile do not coincide; yet the profile can determine what that person encounters next.","r":[{"w":"Artificial Communication, Kapitel „Getting Personal with Algorithms“","d":"Sociology","n":"Algorithmic profiling addresses persons without knowing them in the sense of a personal relationship."},{"w":"Post-scriptum sur les sociétés de contrôle","d":"Philosophy","n":"Data-shaped divisions and continuous modulation describe a recognisability that does not presuppose a complete person."},{"w":"The Address Book","d":"Art","n":"From what people in a found address book say, a portrait of the absent man emerges that offers no complete knowledge of his person."},{"w":"Funes el memorioso","d":"Literature","n":"Funes remembers every detail and precisely for that reason can hardly abstract. Only selection and comparison turn the past into patterns."}]},
  "37-die-information": {"t":"Information","b":"Information arises when a difference has consequences within a particular order.","x":"A data set can be stored; a selection from it becomes informative only for a particular question and a possible connection. The same click can have a different effect in a music recommendation than in a fraud check. Between recording and information lie rules of comparing, weighting and using. These rules are not neutral themselves: they help decide which difference stands out and which goes unconsidered. Platforms automate parts of this selection and use its results for later calculations. Data are certainly a material and technical starting point in this. Information here designates the effect of a difference within an order of observation and action.","r":[{"w":"reflections on information expert systems","d":"Sociology","n":"Information appears not as an existing stock but as an effect of systems that select and combine differences and make them again the precondition of further operations."},{"w":"Meeting the Universe Halfway","d":"Philosophy","n":"Properties and boundaries are not simply given but arise within particular arrangements of observing and acting. What counts as information depends on how differences are cut."},{"w":"In Defense of the Poor Image","d":"Art","n":"The circulation and processing of digital images change their value, legibility and possible use."},{"w":"His Master’s Voice","d":"Literature","n":"A supposed extraterrestrial signal confronts scientists with the question of whether a received difference is information at all. Meaning does not arise from the signal alone."}]},
  "38-die-wahrscheinlichkeit": {"t":"Probability","b":"Probability turns open contingency into an unequally weighted future.","x":"Probability does not remove contingency. It orders it. Between what has happened and what could happen, a distribution of unequal expectations arises. The improbable remains possible but is treated differently from the probable. This makes it possible to work on the future operationally without having to know it. For platforms this shift is decisive: a profile does not have to say what someone will do. It is enough to make different next connections differently probable. Probability turns ignorance into a comparable form. It reduces the future without fixing it – and precisely thereby prepares the selection.","r":[{"w":"works on the social future","d":"Sociology","n":"What is to come already becomes effective in the present through expectations, plans and risks, although it cannot be made present."},{"w":"The Emergence of Probability","d":"Philosophy","n":"Probability does not merely measure uncertainty; it establishes its own way of dealing with events whose individual outcomes remain undetermined."},{"w":"(Des)Ordres","d":"Art","n":"Systematic arrangements of concentric squares are disturbed by programmed deviations; chance works within a designed rule."},{"w":"Gravity’s Rainbow","d":"Literature","n":"Statistics, prediction and probability shift the boundary between observation and control."}]},
  "39-der-algorithmus": {"t":"The Algorithm","b":"Algorithmic ranking distributes unequal chances of the next connection.","x":"On platforms, algorithmic selection procedures order content and suggestions: more or less relevant, visible or invisible, first or later. Such procedures can calculate probabilities from data and translate them into a concrete ranking. This requires prior decisions about what is collected, labelled and counted as success. The power of this ranking lies in preparing the present unequally. What ranks higher is more likely to get the chance to trigger a further connection; what is rarely shown is more easily overlooked. Users can object or choose differently. But their deviations, too, meet an already ordered selection and can enter again as data.","r":[{"w":"The Relevance of Algorithms","d":"Sociology","n":"Algorithms produce socially relevant orders of the visible. Relevance is not a neutral finding of an importance that already exists."},{"w":"On the Existence of Digital Objects","d":"Philosophy","n":"Digital operations bring forth and organise relations; a digital object does not exist independently of the procedures of its actualisation."},{"w":"Myriad (Tulips)","d":"Art","n":"Thousands of hand-labelled photographs of tulips make visible the work of selection and classification on which machine learning rests."},{"w":"No One Is Talking About This","d":"Literature","n":"The novel's stream of the portal shows a present whose perception is already organised by sequence, repetition and technical selection."}]},
  "40-die-empfehlung": {"t":"The Recommendation","b":"The recommendation returns calculated probability as a possible next world.","x":"The recommendation is the moment at which calculation crosses the boundary of the model. A probability that internally was only a number, a weight or a rank now appears as an object in the world: this song, this person, this video, this route. A recommendation does not compel. That is precisely why it is effective. It occupies a possible next place before a search of one's own has begun, and presents this pre-occupation as help. The prediction thereby becomes socially connectable. What was calculated can be chosen, ignored, shared or rejected. Each of these reactions at the same time produces new traces. A recommendation is therefore selection and measuring instrument in one and the same operation.","r":[{"w":"If...Then","d":"Sociology","n":"Algorithmic power shows itself not only in hidden calculation but also in the situations and feelings that algorithms produce for users."},{"w":"Taking Care of Youth and the Generations","d":"Philosophy","n":"Technical orders of attention intervene in cultural memory and in the formation of future expectations."},{"w":"Excellences & Perfections","d":"Art","n":"The work uses the expectations of social platforms as material and shows how self-presentation operates within already established patterns of visibility."},{"w":"The Circle","d":"Literature","n":"Personalisation appears as a service and at the same time as the progressive organisation of what becomes visible, expectable and socially connectable."}]},
  "41-der-feed": {"t":"The Feed","b":"The feed makes prediction the present environment of its next verification.","x":"The feed orders recommendations into a present. It does not simply show what exists but produces a continuous environment from possibilities that have already been weighted. Every selection appears as a now: visible, clickable, open to comment, skippable. Yet this now is calculated from the past and designed for further production of data. The feed does not only predict behaviour; it creates the situation in which the predicted behaviour can take place. Prediction thereby becomes recursive. What is shown influences what is done; what is done changes what can be shown in future. The feed is the present of a prediction that supplies its own preconditions after the fact.","r":[{"w":"The Culture of Connectivity","d":"Sociology","n":"Platforms organise connections as a technical and cultural order of programmed connectivity."},{"w":"works on the digital present","d":"Philosophy","n":"Digital communication produces a highly accelerated order of actualisation in which duration is harder to form."},{"w":"The Clock","d":"Art","n":"Thousands of film fragments are assembled into a present that continually refers to its own time. The stream consists of discrete selections."},{"w":"Weather","d":"Literature","n":"Short observations, news items and reports of crisis form an interrupted present that is linked only in the reading."}]},
  "42-die-plattform": {"t":"The Platform","b":"The platform returns calculated possibilities as environment and processes their consequences.","x":"The platform links profiles, data, calculations, recommendations and feeds into a feedback loop. It observes behaviour, condenses it into patterns and provides selected possibilities as a new environment. This environment influences further actions, which can be observed again. The circle remains open: people, institutions, events and technical errors produce deviations. Where systems additionally generate texts, images or answers, these outputs too become possible next occasions for communication. The platform thus organises not only visibility but changes what can be connected to. Its predictions gain effect by helping to shape the present against which they will later be tested.","r":[{"w":"Metric Power; The Data Gaze","d":"Sociology","n":"Measurement and data analysis do not merely describe society but help bring forth classifications, evaluations and possibilities of action."},{"w":"Contagious Architecture","d":"Philosophy","n":"Algorithmic calculation is not the pure execution of a fully predetermined plan; it produces relations and results whose concrete form is not simply written out."},{"w":"Pulse Room","d":"Art","n":"The audience's bodily signals are translated into a technical environment that changes with every new participation. Observation becomes part of the next situation."},{"w":"The Candy House","d":"Literature","n":"Externalised memory, social networks and algorithmic analysis link the past with a new technical capacity to act."}]},
  "43-die-identitaet": {"t":"Identity","b":"Identity makes change attributable across time.","x":"Identity is not the sameness of a thing with itself. It arises where change can be attributed to the same address across time. A person, a work or an organisation does not remain identical because everything stays the same, but because differences are treated as changes of something recognisable. Identity therefore mediates between experience and expectation. It holds on to enough continuity for deviation to be noticed at all, and enough openness for change to remain attributable. Against platform profiles this distinction becomes important: calculable recognisability is not yet identity. Identity is the form in which change can be continued without dissolving into sameness.","r":[{"w":"Mirrors and Masks","d":"Sociology","n":"Identity appears not as an unchangeable core but as something continually formed in social relationships and biographical transitions."},{"w":"Oneself as Another","d":"Philosophy","n":"The distinction between idem and ipse separates sameness from selfhood: someone can remain the same without remaining alike in all their properties."},{"w":"Album","d":"Art","n":"Masked reconstructions of family photographs separate visible resemblance and personal continuity; here the question of one's own identity comes to the fore."},{"w":"Orlando","d":"Literature","n":"Orlando remains attributable as a figure although centuries, social roles and gender change. Identity becomes narratable through change."}]},
  "44-die-moeglichkeit": {"t":"Possibility","b":"Possibility is the present reachability of a connection not yet actualised.","x":"Possibility is not a stock of invisible worlds behind reality. It is carried along in the present when a connection appears realisable, conceivable or expectable without already being actualised. Every structure limits such possibilities, otherwise no selection would be possible. But it does not necessarily destroy them. A door opens up other connections than a wall; a language other sentences than silence; an institution other decisions than a private whim. Possibility is therefore structured openness. It consists not in the arbitrary but in a present order admitting more than one continuation of its own. The future begins where this more remains practically within reach.","r":[{"w":"Projects and Possibilities: Researching Futures in Action","d":"Sociology","n":"Futures are projected, compared and socially negotiated in present action."},{"w":"Das Prinzip Hoffnung","d":"Philosophy","n":"The not-yet is not mere nothingness but present openness towards possibilities whose realisation depends on concrete conditions."},{"w":"Grapefruit","d":"Art","n":"Instructions propose possible actions. Their form sets conditions without fully determining each actualisation."},{"w":"Der Mann ohne Eigenschaften","d":"Literature","n":"The sense of possibility designates the ability not to confuse reality with its necessity and to see other orders as well."}]},
  "45-die-kontingenz": {"t":"Contingency","b":"Contingency becomes a chance for the future where alternatives remain within reach and structures remain revisable.","x":"Contingent is what is so and could also be otherwise. This possibility of being otherwise is not yet a practically available alternative. An order can be changeable while people have no access to the means of changing it. Expectations, rules and probabilities limit the next connections; in doing so they make decisions possible. What matters is whether such limitations remain open to being worked on. An undetermined situation offers as little hold as one that excludes every deviation. The future needs structures that allow selection and make revision possible. The question of contingency therefore leads on to the question of who can actually recognise, try out and reach alternatives.","r":[{"w":"works on the concept of contingency","d":"Sociology","n":"Contingent is what is neither necessary nor impossible. Structures reduce complexity and at the same time carry along a horizon of other possibilities."},{"w":"works on the repetition of norms","d":"Philosophy","n":"Norms stabilise themselves through repetition without thereby becoming necessary. It is precisely repetition that allows variation and displacement."},{"w":"works with displaced everyday objects and spaces","d":"Art","n":"Familiar forms remain recognisable and at the same time appear as possible otherwise; what is taken for granted becomes unstable."},{"w":"Die unerträgliche Leichtigkeit des Seins","d":"Literature","n":"Because a life cannot be repeated on trial, necessity and chance remain undecidably entangled."}]},
  "46-die-unterbrechung": {"t":"The Interruption","b":"An interruption opens up the future only if something can connect differently after it.","x":"An interruption does not yet produce a future. A failure, a shock or a refusal can just as well destroy a sequence, paralyse it or force it back into its old form. What matters is whether the interruption makes a different continuation connectable. Only then does it change the structure of going on. This holds for conversations, institutions, works of art and technical systems. Interruption is therefore not a romantic counter-figure to order. It is a test of order: can it take in deviation without immediately normalising it or breaking off? The future does not arise in the rupture itself, but in the connections the rupture makes possible.","r":[{"w":"Studies in Ethnomethodology / Breaching Experiments","d":"Sociology","n":"Disruptions of everyday expectations often provoke work of repair. A breach of norms does not automatically produce a new order."},{"w":"The Human Condition","d":"Philosophy","n":"With natality, Arendt links action with the possibility of a beginning. What matters is not the break alone but that a new sequence can begin."},{"w":"Shibboleth","d":"Art","n":"A crack in the floor of Tate Modern makes separation spatially present and forces the institution to perceive architecture and history differently."},{"w":"Waiting for Godot","d":"Literature","n":"Countless interruptions are followed by no real new beginning. The negative case shows that deferral does not necessarily change the structure of continuation."}]},
  "47-das-neue": {"t":"The New","b":"New is what changes what is possible afterwards.","x":"The new is not simply what was not there before. Surprises can remain without consequence, fashions disappear, coincidences fit at once into familiar patterns. Novelty becomes theoretically interesting only where an event changes what is possible afterwards. It shifts expectations, rules, perceptions or relationships and produces forms of connection that were not reachable in the same way before. That is why repetition, too, can be new: when it changes its conditions. The new does not stand outside history but intervenes in its continuation. It is not a property of an object but a difference in the space of the possibilities that follow.","r":[{"w":"The Sociology of an Actor-Network: The Case of the Electric Vehicle","d":"Sociology","n":"A technical innovation becomes effective only within a web of participants, interests and objects."},{"w":"L’individuation à la lumière des notions de forme et d’information","d":"Philosophy","n":"Individuation emerges from metastable situations. The new arises as existing tensions bring forth a form that changes further individuation."},{"w":"Caminhando","d":"Art","n":"The single, unrepeatable cut changes what is still possible afterwards in the continuing form; here the effect on the next step of action comes to the fore."},{"w":"The Dispossessed","d":"Literature","n":"Two differently organised societies show how rules open up and limit possibilities and can be changed in conflict."}]},
  "48-die-freiheit": {"t":"Freedom","b":"Freedom is the ability to take up, reject or reshape structured contingency.","x":"Freedom is neither arbitrariness nor the absence of conditions. Action is always structured: by bodies, language, history, institutions, technology and other people. Freedom therefore shows itself not in beginning without preconditions, but in being able to deal with them differently. An expectation can be adopted, rejected, shifted or reshaped; a rule can be followed, interpreted or made the object of new rules. Freedom needs possibilities, but also abilities and conditions to perceive them and work on them. It is a structured disposal over contingency. Unfreedom begins where alternatives formally exist but no longer become practically connectable.","r":[{"w":"works on habitus and practice","d":"Sociology","n":"Practices are neither free of history nor mechanically determined; they arise as structured improvisations within unequally distributed spaces of possibility."},{"w":"Pour une morale de l’ambiguïté","d":"Philosophy","n":"Freedom is situated. It is realised not outside conditions and other freedoms but in engaging with them."},{"w":"Arte Útil","d":"Art","n":"Art is examined as a social possibility of action. What matters is whether situations arise in which those involved can actually act differently."},{"w":"Invisible Man","d":"Literature","n":"Room for action increasingly arises where the narrator refuses to be wholly absorbed into the identities offered by others."}]},
  "49-die-zukunft": {"t":"The Future","b":"The future is the preserved room to go on otherwise than is already expected.","x":"Later events will occur, however open an order is today. In this project, future means more: the present room for continuations that are not yet fully expected or prepared. Every order must restrict possibilities in order to be able to act. It endangers its own capacity for a future when it can treat deviations only as errors and can no longer reach alternatives. Openness consists neither in the arbitrary nor in the assurance of a better outcome. It shows itself in whether experiences, objections and new conditions can change a continuation. The future of the stanza is this preserved surplus of going on otherwise over expectation that is already organised.","r":[{"w":"Time: The Modern and Postmodern Experience","d":"Sociology","n":"An extended present draws ever larger parts of the future into present decisions through planning and expectation."},{"w":"Process and Reality","d":"Philosophy","n":"Reality consists of events of becoming; creativity designates the possibility that a new concrete order arises from what is given."},{"w":"Future Library","d":"Art","n":"The work organises the future very precisely over a hundred years and at the same time keeps open which texts, readers and social conditions will one day come together."},{"w":"Story of Your Life; What’s Expected of Us","d":"Literature","n":"The stories examine how action changes when the future appears foreseeable, and distinguish knowledge of what is to come from the possibility of action."}]}
};
export const UEBUNGEN_EN = {
  "100": {"n":"A surface from fingerprints","a":["With one finger set a few prints side by side.","In a second place set many prints densely on top of each other.","Compare the loose and the dense surface."],"m":"Paper, finger paint, damp cloth","f":"Where do you still recognise single prints?"},
  "101": {"n":"Scratch out light marks","a":["Colour a small area firmly with the light crayon.","Cover it with a layer of black crayon.","Scratch lines through the black layer with the wooden stick so that the light colour shows."],"m":"Paper, light and black oil pastel, wooden stick","f":"Which marks stand out particularly on the dark ground?"},
  "102": {"n":"Let paint run","a":["Put a drop of paint on the paper at the top edge.","Lift this edge slightly so that the drop runs downwards.","Turn the sheet at most three times to try new directions of flow. Do not trace the marks."],"m":"Paper, liquid watercolour, brush, base","f":"Where did a turn change the path of the paint most?"},
  "103": {"n":"A shape from dots","a":["Choose a simple shape, for example a circle.","Build it exclusively from dots.","Set some dots close together and others far apart. Vary their size too."],"m":"Paper, felt pen","f":"From which spacing do the dots look like a continuous surface to you?"},
  "104": {"n":"Print with a foam plate","a":["Press lines into the plate with the ballpoint pen without piercing it.","Roll on a thin layer of ink. Lay paper on it, press it down and peel it off.","Make a second print with a little more or less ink."],"m":"Foam printing plate, ballpoint pen, water-based printing ink, roller, 2 sheets","f":"Which lines are clearer in which print?"},
  "105": {"n":"Reassemble painted paper","a":["Paint three pieces of paper differently, perhaps with blots, stripes and a single-colour area. Let them dry.","Cut a few shapes from them.","Lay the shapes on a new sheet and glue them into a picture."],"m":"Paper, watercolours, brush, water, scissors, glue","f":"Which painted mark stands out particularly in the new picture?"},
  "106": {"n":"Cut out shapes and shift them","a":["Cut out a few shapes without drawing first.","Lay them on the base and try three different arrangements.","Choose one arrangement. You can leave it lying or glue it down."],"m":"Coloured paper, scissors, a sheet as a base, glue if you wish","f":"Which shift changed the picture most?"},
  "107": {"n":"Weave on cardboard","a":["Cut six small slits into the cardboard at the top and six opposite at the bottom.","Wind wool around the cardboard six times. Lay it in the slits and knot the ends at the back.","Lead the second wool across, alternately over and under the stretched threads.","Weave back in the next row and swap over and under."],"m":"A postcard-sized piece of cardboard, wool in 2 colours, scissors","f":"Which pattern appears from the alternation of the two threads?"},
  "108": {"n":"Lines of salt","a":["Sprinkle a few lines of salt on the tray with the spoon.","Push them together into a surface with the card.","Draw new lines through the salt with the tip of the spoon."],"m":"Dark tray, salt, spoon, old plastic card","f":"Which shape do you like enough to leave it standing for a while?"},
  "109": {"n":"Make a sheet stand up","a":["Fold over part of the sheet at the bottom as a base.","Change the upper part with folds and cuts.","Stand the sheet up and adjust the base until it stands by itself."],"m":"A sheet of paper or thin cardboard, scissors","f":"Which change made your shape more stable?"},
  "110": {"n":"Slot cardboard together","a":["Cut out two rectangles. Cut a narrow slit into each from the edge to about the middle.","Slot the two rectangles into each other at right angles.","Add a third piece of cardboard with a matching slit."],"m":"Thin cardboard, scissors","f":"How deep do the slits have to be for the parts to hold well?"},
  "111": {"n":"Shape clay with six movements","a":["Press a piece of clay flat, roll it and stretch it out.","Fold it, pinch one spot together and press hollows into it with a fingertip.","After each movement look briefly at the change."],"m":"Modelling clay, base","f":"Which movement leaves the clearest trace?"},
  "112": {"n":"Build a shape twice","a":["Build a simple shape that stands by itself.","Press it flat or pull it strongly apart.","From this changed piece build a second shape that also stands."],"m":"Modelling clay, base","f":"What in the second shape still recalls the first?"},
  "113": {"n":"Join five found objects","a":["Lay the found objects in front of you and try out which go together.","Join them into a small shape, for example by wrapping, clamping or slotting.","At least two joints should hold without glue."],"m":"5 small found objects without sharp edges, string, clips","f":"Which joint suits the shape and material of the parts particularly well?"},
  "114": {"n":"Rub a surface four times","a":["Lay the first sheet on the surface and rub lightly over it with the flat pencil lead.","Repeat on the other sheets with a little more, medium and firm pressure.","Compare the four rubbings."],"m":"4 sheets of thin paper, soft pencil, a firm textured surface","f":"At which pressure does the pattern become clearest?"},
  "115": {"n":"Make a print","a":["Spread a thin layer of paint on the plate.","Draw lines through the still wet paint with the cotton bud.","Lay paper on it, press it down with your hand and lift it off carefully."],"m":"Smooth plastic plate without sharp edges, gouache, brush, cotton bud, paper","f":"Which marks on the paper are different from those on the plate?"},
  "116": {"n":"Use a shape and its hole","a":["Fold a sheet. Cut half a shape out at the fold and open both parts.","Lay the cut-out shape on the base and paint around its edge.","Next to it lay the paper with the hole and paint in the opening."],"m":"Paper, scissors, wax crayon, a sheet as a base","f":"How do the two resulting shapes differ?"},
  "117": {"n":"With a wet and an almost dry brush","a":["Moisten a small area of the paper with water and set paint into it.","Pick up the same paint with the brush and wipe off plenty of water on the cloth.","Paint with the almost dry brush on a dry area of the sheet."],"m":"Watercolour paper, watercolour, brush, water, cloth","f":"How do the edges and marks of the two areas differ?"},
  "118": {"n":"Lay coloured layers over each other","a":["Cut a simple shape from each sheet of film.","Lay the shapes on white paper so that they partly overlap.","Shift them and look at the places with two or three layers."],"m":"3 transparent films of different colours, white paper, scissors","f":"Which colours do you see only where the films overlap?"},
  "119": {"n":"Draw the same subject with more time","a":["Draw the object on the first sheet for 30 seconds.","Draw it on the second sheet for three minutes.","Draw it on the third sheet for ten minutes. Always use the same pen."],"m":"An object, 3 sheets, pen, timer","f":"What does the longest drawing show in addition, and what do you like about the shortest?"},
  "120": {"n":"Twelve small variations","a":["Choose a simple shape, such as a leaf.","Draw twelve small variations of it. Compared with the starting shape change only one feature each time, such as size, direction or edge.","Compare the twelve variations."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"Which small change makes the biggest difference?"},
  "121": {"n":"Describe what can be seen","a":["Look at your work for one minute.","Write down five visible things, for example «three red dots» or «a line at the edge».","Leave out judgements and assumed meanings for now."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pen, timer","f":"Which detail had you not noticed while making it?"},
  "122": {"n":"Discover more at a second look","a":["Look briefly at your work and note five things you notice.","Look at it for another 30 seconds.","Note up to five additional observations."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pen, timer","f":"What did you notice only by looking longer?"},
  "123": {"n":"Describe colours, shapes and lines","a":["Write one sentence about the colours of your work.","Write a second sentence about the shapes and a third about the lines.","Compare what you described with the three sentences."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pen","f":"Which sentence says the most about the work, for you?"},
  "124": {"n":"First and second impression","a":["Look briefly at your work and write down three words for your first impression.","Then look at the work for one minute.","Check the words: which still fit, which would you replace?"],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pen, timer","f":"Which detail changed your impression, if it changed at all?"},
  "125": {"n":"Find an unplanned spot","a":["Look for a spot that turned out differently than planned.","Look at it for a while on its own and then within the whole picture.","Decide whether you want to keep it, change it or leave it for now."],"m":"A work of your own","f":"What speaks for your decision?"},
  "126": {"n":"Follow your own gaze","a":["Look away briefly and then at your work again.","Note the spot you look at first and the next two spots.","Sketch the picture roughly and connect these spots in their order with arrows."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pen","f":"What attracted your gaze first?"},
  "127": {"n":"Look closely and from further away","a":["Look at the work from about a hand's width, an arm's length and a few steps away.","For each distance note one thing that particularly strikes you.","Compare your three observations."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pen","f":"What do you see only close up, what better from further away?"},
  "128": {"n":"Look at a picture in four positions","a":["First look at the work in its current position.","Turn it one, two and three quarter turns in succession.","Look at it briefly in each position without adding anything."],"m":"A work of your own","f":"Which position would you choose for the work now?"},
  "129": {"n":"Find a small picture in the large one","a":["Lay the paper strips on the work as a small frame.","Move them until you find a crop you like on its own.","Photograph only this crop."],"m":"A work of your own, 4 paper strips, smartphone","f":"What stands out more in the crop than in the whole picture?"},
  "130": {"n":"Find a short title","a":["Look at your work closely.","Give it a title of no more than four words.","Then look at the work again with this title in mind."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pen","f":"Does the title direct your gaze to something in particular?"},
  "131": {"n":"Find three verbs","a":["Look at your work and search for words that express a movement or a change.","Write down three, for example grow, fall or circle.","Point to the spots that fit your words."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pen","f":"Which word best describes what seems to be happening in the picture, for you?"},
  "132": {"n":"Find five fitting words","a":["Write down five words about the look of your work, such as dense, open, angular, soft or bright.","Leave out judgements like «beautiful» and «ugly».","For each word point to a matching spot in the picture."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pen","f":"Which word fits particularly many spots?"},
  "133": {"n":"Look for traces of making","a":["Imagine you had not made the work yourself.","Look for up to five traces, such as a brushstroke, a glued edge or a fold.","For each trace note which step of the work probably produced it."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pen","f":"Which step of the work can you recognise particularly well in the result?"},
  "134": {"n":"Find three decisions again","a":["Look for three spots where you consciously decided something, such as a colour, a cut or a distance.","Note each decision briefly.","Remember what you did afterwards."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pen","f":"Which decision influenced the rest of your way the most?"},
  "135": {"n":"Deal with chance","a":["Look for up to three spots that were not planned.","Write for each spot: keep, change or still open.","Leave the work unchanged for now."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pen","f":"Which unplanned spot do you want to keep deliberately?"},
  "136": {"n":"Find your own rule again","a":["Remember the rule, for example «only three colours».","Look for up to three spots where you can clearly recognise the rule.","Imagine one of these spots without the rule."],"m":"A work of your own that came about with a rule","f":"What might you have done differently at this spot?"},
  "137": {"n":"Find strong differences","a":["Look for up to three spots where strong differences meet.","Note the differences, such as light and dark, large and small or smooth and rough.","Compare how strongly these spots catch your attention."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pen","f":"Which difference catches your eye first?"},
  "138": {"n":"Follow repetitions","a":["Look for a shape, colour or line that occurs several times.","Draw its sequence in simplified form on a new sheet.","Mark larger gaps or interruptions."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pen","f":"Where does the sequence change?"},
  "139": {"n":"Swap shape and background","a":["Look at a shape that strikes you at once.","Then look at the area around it as if it were a shape itself.","Switch between the two ways of seeing a few times."],"m":"A work of your own","f":"Where does the switch succeed, where hardly at all?"},
  "140": {"n":"Which colour stands out first","a":["Look briefly at your work and note the colour you notice first.","Then look for the next most noticeable colour. Repeat for the remaining colours.","Compare your order with the size of the areas of colour."],"m":"A coloured work of your own, paper, pen","f":"Does a small area of colour strike you more than a large one?"},
  "141": {"n":"A small picture in grey","a":["Draw a rectangle about the size of a matchbox.","Transfer only the most important areas of your work into it.","Use white, light grey, medium grey, dark grey and almost black. Leave out small details."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pencil","f":"Which parts of your work do you recognise in the small drawing?"},
  "142": {"n":"Sum up a picture with five lines","a":["Look at the most important directions in your work.","On a new sheet draw no more than five lines that show these directions.","Leave out colours and small details."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pen","f":"Which of your lines is particularly important for recognising it?"},
  "143": {"n":"Compare two versions","a":["Lay the two works side by side.","Note three visible differences, such as in colour, size or arrangement.","Also look for something they have in common that you did not notice at once."],"m":"2 works of your own on the same task, paper, pen","f":"Which of your decisions led to one of these differences?"},
  "144": {"n":"Change exactly one thing","a":["Look at the work and choose exactly one spot you want to change.","Decide what you want to change there, such as lengthening a line or darkening an area.","Make only this change and put the tool down."],"m":"A work of your own, a matching pen or matching paint","f":"What changes through this one intervention?"},
  "145": {"n":"Think a shape away","a":["Choose a striking shape or line.","Imagine it were not there.","Try this with two more spots."],"m":"A work of your own","f":"Which omission would change the work the most, for you?"},
  "146": {"n":"Think a shape twice","a":["Choose a shape or line in your work.","Imagine the same shape were there a second time.","Try two different places for it in your mind."],"m":"A work of your own","f":"In which place would the repetition change the picture the most?"},
  "147": {"n":"Look for a fitting place","a":["Try three places or heights, for example on the table, on a shelf and leaning against a wall.","Look at the work briefly in each place.","Choose the place where you want to leave it for now."],"m":"A work of your own","f":"What about this place suits the work, for you?"},
  "148": {"n":"Photograph the work","a":["Photograph the work once as straight on as possible.","Take two more photos from a slightly different distance or angle.","Compare them with the original. Choose the photo that shows shapes and proportions most similarly."],"m":"A work of your own, smartphone or camera","f":"What does even this photo show differently from the original?"},
  "149": {"n":"Instructions for rebuilding","a":["Describe in a few steps how someone could rebuild the arrangement of your work.","Name shapes, colours, sizes and positions, for example «a small red circle at the top right».","Cover the original and check whether you can imagine the picture from your instructions."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pen","f":"Which instruction would you have to make more precise?"},
  "150": {"n":"Find a new beginning","a":["Choose a detail of your work, such as a colour, line or small shape.","Transfer this detail to a new sheet.","Leave it as a possible beginning for a further work."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pens","f":"Starting from this detail, what would you try next?"},
  "151": {"n":"Describe colours and collect ideas","a":["For each colour write down three visible features, such as light, strong or blotchy.","Next to it write three things it reminds you of, such as forest, holidays or a piece of clothing.","Compare the two lists."],"m":"3 coloured objects, paper, pen","f":"Which words describe the appearance, which a memory or idea?"},
  "152": {"n":"Colours for a word","a":["Choose a word, for example «loud», «cool» or «festive».","Paint five small patches of colour that fit it for you.","Add a sixth colour that at first does not fit."],"m":"Coloured pencils, paper","f":"Can the sixth colour fit your word too? If so, how?"},
  "153": {"n":"Find signs in everyday life","a":["Look for five signs around you, such as arrow, heart, star, cross or tick.","Draw them.","For each note two situations in which it could occur and mean something different."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"For which sign do the meanings differ particularly strongly?"},
  "154": {"n":"Choose objects by their look","a":["Choose five objects whose shape, material or surface interests you.","Lay them side by side.","Order them by their appearance, for example round things together, elongated things next to them."],"m":"5 small everyday objects","f":"Which two objects go together visually although they are used quite differently?"},
  "155": {"n":"Describe materials by their qualities","a":["Take each piece in your hand and feel its surface. Bend or press it carefully if possible.","Write down two qualities for each, such as smooth, pliable, soft or springy.","Use no material names like wood or fabric."],"m":"6 pieces of material, paper, pen","f":"Which quality did you notice only by touching?"},
  "156": {"n":"Observe movements","a":["Watch five everyday movements, such as a door, a hand or a curtain.","Write down a fitting word for each, for example slow, jerky or circling.","Sketch one of the movements with a few lines."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"Which movement was the hardest to describe with one word?"},
  "157": {"n":"An effect with another colour","a":["Choose a quality, for example «calm» or «wild», and a colour that fits it for you.","Draw a small picture with it that shows this quality.","Next to it try it with a colour that hardly fits for you. Change shapes and distances to do so."],"m":"Paper, coloured pencils","f":"Which shapes did you choose for the second picture?"},
  "158": {"n":"One mark after another","a":["Set a first line or blot on the sheet.","Look briefly. Add a second mark, perhaps next to it, across it or as a continuation.","Repeat this alternation of looking and adding ten times."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"Which mark particularly influenced your next step?"},
  "159": {"n":"Continue a quick picture slowly","a":["Paint for two minutes without a predetermined subject. Let the sheet dry.","Choose a small area and cut it out.","Work on slowly only on this crop, perhaps with additional lines or colour."],"m":"Paper, watercolour, broad brush, water, scissors, timer","f":"What did you take over from the quick picture?"},
  "160": {"n":"The same sign in three surroundings","a":["Draw the same sign three times at the same size, for example a heart.","Design different surroundings for each, such as flowers, a price tag or a speech bubble.","Leave the sign itself unchanged each time."],"m":"Paper, coloured pencils","f":"What could the sign mean in each of the three pictures?"},
  "161": {"n":"Repeat a chance curve","a":["Let a string fall onto the paper from a low height.","Trace its course carefully.","Next to it draw the same curve freehand several times, with spacing as even as possible."],"m":"String, paper, pencil","f":"What looks regular through the repetition although the first line came about by chance?"},
  "162": {"n":"Make a clear shape from soft fabric","a":["Choose a shape, for example circle, triangle or square.","Fold or roll the cloth and tie it with the string if needed.","Put it down and try to keep the chosen shape recognisable."],"m":"A small cloth, string","f":"Where does the fabric give way again?"},
  "163": {"n":"Stay with the first choice","a":["Quickly choose one colour and one tool.","Work with those only for five minutes.","Look at the result before you take another colour or another tool."],"m":"Paper, coloured pens or paints and brush, timer","f":"What could you do with this one combination?"},
  "164": {"n":"Stop after two minutes","a":["Lay out the material and set the timer to two minutes.","Start painting at once and decide about colours, directions and areas as you go.","Stop at the signal and leave the picture unchanged."],"m":"Paper, watercolour, broad brush, water, timer","f":"Which decision did you make particularly quickly?"},
  "165": {"n":"Make a shape stand out","a":["Spread the ten shapes on the base. Choose one that should stand out.","Leave this shape where it is. Move the others closer to it or further away.","Also try a coloured sheet as a new background."],"m":"10 paper shapes, a sheet as a base, 2 sheets of different colours","f":"In which arrangement does your chosen shape stand out most?"},
  "166": {"n":"Use a chance curve as a template","a":["Let the string fall three times and trace its course each time.","Trace one of the curves onto tracing paper. Hatch the back with the pencil.","Lay the tracing paper on a new sheet. Go over the curve with some pressure so that it appears on the sheet below.","Transfer the curve at least five times in all."],"m":"String, paper, soft pencil, tracing paper","f":"From when do you recognise the recurring curve as a pattern?"},
  "167": {"n":"Five kinds of lines","a":["Write down five words: flowing, angular, swirling, light and still.","For each word draw a matching line, such as an arc or a zigzag.","Repeat each kind of line a few times in a small field of its own."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"In which field does the kind of line fit the word particularly well, for you?"},
  "168": {"n":"A work just for you","a":["Decide that you do not have to show this work to anyone for now.","Draw for ten minutes with the colours and shapes you feel like.","Look at it in between and decide each time what you want to add."],"m":"Paper, coloured pencils, timer","f":"Did you try something you would otherwise rather have left out?"},
  "169": {"n":"Five sheets in two minutes each","a":["Lay out all five sheets. Work sitting or standing, whichever suits you.","Paint on each sheet for exactly two minutes without deciding on a subject first.","Switch to the next sheet at once after each signal. At the end lay all five side by side."],"m":"5 sheets, watercolours, broad brush, water, timer","f":"Which colours, shapes or movements recur on several sheets?"},
  "170": {"n":"Paint without a fixed subject","a":["Begin with one colour and a first mark.","Look briefly and decide what you want to add next to it, above it or after it.","Repeat until you want to stop. No recognisable object needs to emerge."],"m":"Paper, watercolours, brush, water","f":"Which spot led you to a next step?"},
  "171": {"n":"Draw without explanation","a":["Draw whatever comes to mind for ten minutes.","Put the pens down and look at the sheet briefly.","Leave it without a title for now and do without an explanation."],"m":"Paper, pens, timer","f":"What do you notice when you just look?"},
  "172": {"n":"Look, and draw rarely","a":["Look at the object for five minutes.","After every 30 seconds set exactly one line or mark on the sheet.","Between the marks keep looking at the object."],"m":"An object, paper, pen, timer","f":"Which detail did you discover while waiting?"},
  "173": {"n":"Draw movements","a":["Move your hand or arm in turn softly, angularly, chaotically, lightly and very slowly.","After each movement draw lines on a new sheet for one minute that fit it.","Lay the five drawings side by side."],"m":"Some space, 5 sheets, pen, timer","f":"Which drawing reminds you most clearly of your movement?"},
  "174": {"n":"Draw a process","a":["Choose a simple process, for example making tea.","Draw it for five minutes with words, circles and arrows. Connect what belongs together or follows on.","Look at the drawing and add a missing step if you notice one."],"m":"Large paper, pen, timer","f":"While drawing, did you notice a step you had not thought of before?"},
  "175": {"n":"Distinguish observation and interpretation","a":["Write six short sentences about your work.","Mark what is directly visible, such as «The line is jagged», and what you interpret, such as «The line looks angry».","Split sentences that contain both into two separate statements."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pen","f":"Which statement was the hardest to assign?"},
  "176": {"n":"Look at colour and arrangement","a":["Choose two striking colours of your work. Note one impression for each, such as calm or loud.","Look more closely: how large, how light and where are the areas of colour?","Note what apart from the colour could contribute to your impression."],"m":"A coloured work of your own, paper, pen","f":"What role could the arrangement play in your impression?"},
  "177": {"n":"Allow several meanings","a":["Choose a striking sign or shape in your work.","Write down three different things that could be seen in it.","Look at the shape in turn with each of these three ideas in mind."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pen","f":"With which idea do you perceive the shape differently?"},
  "178": {"n":"Direct your gaze to another shape","a":["Choose the shape you notice first and look at it for two minutes.","Then deliberately direct your gaze to another shape.","Now look at the remaining parts as its surroundings."],"m":"A work of your own, timer","f":"What do you perceive differently at the second look?"},
  "179": {"n":"Three minutes in front of your own picture","a":["Set the timer to three minutes and look at your work.","Leave it unchanged during this time. Do not photograph or describe it yet.","Afterwards note up to three things you noticed anew."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pen, timer","f":"Which observation only came after a while?"},
  "180": {"n":"Distinguish chance and decision","a":["Choose five striking spots in your work.","For each note: came about rather by chance, rather deliberately designed, or both.","Think about whether you later deliberately left a chance mark as it was."],"m":"A work of your own, paper, pen","f":"At which spot did chance and decision come together?"},
  "001": {"n":"Collect ten colours","a":["Look around you for ten different colours.","Lay the objects or pieces of paper side by side.","Arrange them so that similar colours lie together."],"m":"10 small coloured objects or pieces of paper","f":"Which colour was the hardest to find?"},
  "002": {"n":"Almost the same colours","a":["Look for five similar shades, for example five different greens.","Lay them in a row from the lightest to the darkest shade.","Compare each two neighbouring pieces."],"m":"5 pieces of paper in similar shades","f":"Between which two shades do you see the smallest difference?"},
  "003": {"n":"Find colour pairs","a":["Look for these three colour pairs: red and green, blue and orange, yellow and violet.","Cut a small piece of each colour.","Lay the two colours of a pair directly side by side."],"m":"Coloured paper or coloured packaging, scissors","f":"Which pair stands out most to you?"},
  "004": {"n":"Warm and cool colours","a":["Collect five pieces of colour that feel warm to you and five that feel cool.","Form two groups from them.","Take another piece of colour. Place it first with one group, then with the other."],"m":"Coloured paper or coloured packaging, scissors","f":"Does the extra piece of colour look the same in both groups?"},
  "005": {"n":"Colour in different light","a":["Put the object by the window, in a shady corner and under a lamp.","Look at it briefly in each place.","Note each time whether its colour looks lighter, darker or different."],"m":"A coloured object, paper, pen","f":"Where do you see the biggest difference?"},
  "006": {"n":"Colours from packaging","a":["Cut out ten small coloured pieces.","Lay them side by side and look for things they have in common, such as similar brightness.","Then sort them into groups without writing down colour names."],"m":"Old magazines or packaging, scissors","f":"How do you recognise which pieces belong together?"},
  "007": {"n":"Collect lines","a":["Look around you for ten different lines, for example on a cable, a table edge or a leaf.","Draw only the line from each find.","Leave some space between the lines."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"Which line would you like to use in a drawing of your own?"},
  "008": {"n":"Show movements with lines","a":["Watch three movements, for example a hand, a door or a leaf in the wind.","Draw each movement with two or three lines.","Draw the path of the movement without depicting the whole object."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"In which drawing can you recognise the movement best?"},
  "009": {"n":"Sort shapes","a":["Look for ten shapes around you and draw their outlines quickly.","Assign them to three groups: regular shapes like circle and rectangle; free shapes like the outline of a leaf; shapes that are hard to place.","Compare the three groups."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"Which shape fits into more than one group for you?"},
  "010": {"n":"Draw only the outline","a":["Choose five things around you.","Draw only the outer outline of each. Leave out everything inside.","Look at the drawings without naming the things."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"Which shape could also belong to a completely different object?"},
  "011": {"n":"Rub four surfaces","a":["Look for four different surfaces, such as wood, a coin, cardboard and a leaf with veins.","Lay paper on each and rub over it with the pencil lead held flat.","Lay the four patterns side by side."],"m":"Thin paper, soft pencil, 4 firm surfaces","f":"In which pattern can you recognise the surface particularly well?"},
  "012": {"n":"Order materials by touch","a":["Feel the five materials one after another with your fingers.","Order them from as smooth as possible to as rough as possible.","Then look at their surfaces."],"m":"5 pieces of material, such as fabric, sponge, cardboard, wood and metal","f":"Do appearance and feel go together for you?"},
  "013": {"n":"Change paper in five ways","a":["Leave the first piece flat.","Fold the second, tear the edge of the third, roll the fourth and crumple the fifth.","Lay all five pieces side by side."],"m":"5 equally sized pieces of the same paper","f":"With which piece has the effect of the paper changed the most?"},
  "014": {"n":"Draw edges","a":["Look for five different edges, such as on a book, fabric, a leaf, torn paper and two overlapping things.","Draw each edge as a single line.","Keep looking back at the edge as you draw."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"Which of the lines differs most from the others?"},
  "015": {"n":"Draw the spaces between","a":["Lay the objects on the table with some distance between them.","Look for five gaps between them.","Draw only the shapes of these gaps."],"m":"Paper, pen, a few small objects","f":"Which gap looks like a shape of its own on the paper?"},
  "016": {"n":"Find similar shapes","a":["Choose a shape from your surroundings, for example a circle.","Look for six places where this shape occurs.","Draw the six finds small, side by side."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"What do the shapes have in common, even though they look different?"},
  "017": {"n":"Almost mirror images","a":["Look for three things whose left and right halves resemble each other, such as a leaf or a window.","Draw them roughly.","Mark one place on each where the two halves differ."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"Which deviation stands out most to you?"},
  "018": {"n":"Draw stains and cracks","a":["Look for five traces that came about by chance, such as stains, cracks or worn spots.","Draw each trace as a shape on its own sheet.","Lay the five drawings side by side in a way you like."],"m":"5 small sheets, pen","f":"Which trace would suit you best as a starting point for a picture?"},
  "019": {"n":"Draw shadows","a":["Light an object from the side so that its shadow falls on the paper.","Trace the outline of the shadow.","Repeat with other objects or positions of the light until you have five outlines."],"m":"Paper, pen, small objects, desk lamp","f":"In which shadow can you barely recognise the object any more?"},
  "020": {"n":"Seeing through and reflections","a":["Look for five places where you see something through a surface or as a reflection.","Look through a window, into a mirror or at a shiny spoon, for example.","Sketch briefly what you see in each case."],"m":"Paper, pen, glass surfaces or reflective things around you","f":"Where do you see the surface and something behind or inside it at the same time?"},
  "021": {"n":"Find connections","a":["Look for five connections, such as a knot, a button, a zip, a strip of tape and a door hinge.","Draw only the spot where the parts are held together.","Write briefly next to each how it holds."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"Which connection could you try with your own material?"},
  "022": {"n":"Three crops of a view","a":["Form a small rectangular frame with your thumbs and index fingers.","Look through it at the view.","Find three different crops by moving the frame or changing its size."],"m":"A view in the room or out of the window, your hands","f":"What do you notice first in each crop?"},
  "023": {"n":"Look from five distances","a":["Look at the object very close up, from an arm's length and from one, two and three steps away.","At each position note three things you see.","Compare your notes."],"m":"An object, paper, pen","f":"Which detail can no longer be seen from the greatest distance?"},
  "024": {"n":"Draw from five sides","a":["Look at the object from above, below, front, back and at an angle. Turn it in your hand to do so.","From each direction draw only its outline for 30 seconds.","Lay the five views side by side."],"m":"A small object, paper, pen, timer","f":"From which direction is the object easiest to recognise?"},
  "025": {"n":"From light to dark","a":["Look for five pieces of paper: a very light one, a very dark one and three in between.","Lay them in a row from the lightest to the darkest piece.","Compare the brightness of neighbouring pieces."],"m":"5 pieces of paper of different brightness","f":"Where is the difference in brightness particularly large?"},
  "026": {"n":"Sounds as lines","a":["Listen to the sounds around you for two minutes.","For five sounds draw one line each, perhaps short, shaky, wavy or broken.","Leave out the sources of the sounds; so do not draw a bird, for instance."],"m":"Paper, pen, timer","f":"Which of your lines fits the sound you heard best, for you?"},
  "027": {"n":"Feel, then draw from memory","a":["Close your eyes and feel an object with both hands.","Put it out of sight. Open your eyes and draw its shape and surface from memory.","Repeat with the other two objects. Then compare the drawings and the things."],"m":"3 small objects without sharp edges, paper, pen","f":"Which quality could you feel well but draw with difficulty?"},
  "028": {"n":"Compare five papers","a":["Bend each paper slightly and order the papers from soft to stiff.","Feel the surfaces and order them from smooth to rough.","Hold each paper up to the window and compare how much light comes through."],"m":"5 different papers, such as newspaper, copy paper, cardboard, baking paper and gift wrap","f":"Which paper would you choose for folding, which for drawing?"},
  "029": {"n":"Natural shapes and made shapes","a":["Look for five shapes from nature and five on made things.","Draw them small, for example a leaf and a spoon.","Connect two drawings whose shapes resemble each other although they come from different groups."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"In what way are the two shapes alike?"},
  "030": {"n":"Remember a colour","a":["Look at the colour of the object for 20 seconds.","Put the object out of sight. Choose the closest coloured pencil and paint a small patch of colour.","Bring the object back and compare the colours."],"m":"A coloured object, several coloured pencils, paper, timer","f":"Is your patch lighter, darker or similar to the original?"},
  "031": {"n":"Draw a small detail large","a":["Choose a small detail, such as a leaf vein, a corner or a crop of a picture.","Draw this detail so large that it almost fills your sheet.","Leave out everything outside the chosen detail."],"m":"A picture or object, paper, pen","f":"In the large drawing, can you still tell where the detail comes from?"},
  "032": {"n":"Draw with fewer shapes","a":["First draw the subject with ten simple shapes.","Draw this version again next to it with five shapes. Combine parts or leave them out.","From that, draw a third version with only three shapes."],"m":"A photo with several details, paper, pen","f":"Which shape had to be kept until the very end, for you?"},
  "033": {"n":"Turn and add","a":["Turn the drawing a quarter turn and add exactly one shape.","Turn it another quarter turn and add one more shape.","Repeat a third time."],"m":"A drawing of your own, pen","f":"In which position was it easiest for you to add something?"},
  "034": {"n":"Mirror a picture","a":["Draw the original the other way round: what is on the left goes to the right.","Compare the two versions.","In the mirrored drawing change exactly one shape, for example its size."],"m":"A simple drawing, paper, pen","f":"What do you notice anew because of this one change?"},
  "035": {"n":"Choose three crops","a":["Lay the paper strips as a frame on your work.","Move the frame and look for three different crops.","Photograph each crop so that only the framed area can be seen."],"m":"A work of your own, 4 paper strips, smartphone","f":"Which crop looks like a picture of its own to you?"},
  "036": {"n":"Design the space around a shape","a":["Draw a simple closed shape in the middle of the sheet.","Leave its inside white and design only the area around it.","Fill this area with colours, lines or patterns."],"m":"Paper, pencil, coloured pencils","f":"How does the white shape look because of its surroundings?"},
  "037": {"n":"Try other colours","a":["Draw the most important shapes of your original on a new sheet.","Keep the shapes roughly the same size and in the same place.","Colour each shape with a different colour than in the original."],"m":"A coloured drawing of your own, paper, coloured pencils","f":"What looks different because of the new colours?"},
  "038": {"n":"One colour on two backgrounds","a":["Cut two equally sized squares from the same paper.","Place one of the squares on each background.","Push the backgrounds side by side and compare the squares."],"m":"A piece of coloured paper, 2 sheets of different colours, scissors","f":"Do the two squares look the same or different to you?"},
  "039": {"n":"Watch an afterimage","a":["Lay both papers side by side.","Look at the middle of the coloured paper for about 20 seconds. Blink as normal.","Then look at the white paper and watch whether a colour appears there briefly."],"m":"A piece of strongly coloured paper, white paper, timer","f":"What do you see on the white paper, if anything appears at all?"},
  "040": {"n":"Connect two colours","a":["Paint a patch with the first colour on the left and one with the second on the right.","Paint seven more patches in between. For each, mix a little more of the second colour into the first.","Look at the whole row of colours."],"m":"2 watercolours, brush, water, mixing surface, paper","f":"Between which patches is the change of colour particularly clear?"},
  "041": {"n":"Four pictures with four colours","a":["Paint a few simple shapes on each sheet.","Use the same four colours on every sheet.","From sheet to sheet, change where the colours lie and how much space they get."],"m":"4 small sheets, 4 coloured pencils","f":"Which of the four pictures feels calmest to you?"},
  "042": {"n":"A picture from paper surfaces","a":["Look in the photo for the five largest or most striking areas of colour.","Cut a roughly matching shape from coloured paper for each.","Lay them on the base as in the photo and glue them down. Leave out small details."],"m":"A photo, coloured paper, scissors, glue, a sheet as a base","f":"Which area helps you most to recognise the photo?"},
  "043": {"n":"An object in three versions","a":["Draw the object with the details you notice.","Draw it again next to it and leave out small details.","Draw a third version from no more than three simple shapes or lines."],"m":"A small object, paper, pen","f":"In which version can you still recognise the object well?"},
  "044": {"n":"A subject from five paper shapes","a":["Choose a simple subject, for example a tree or a cup.","Cut out no more than five shapes for it.","Put them together into your subject and glue them down."],"m":"Coloured paper, scissors, glue, a sheet as a base","f":"Which shape is particularly important for recognising it?"},
  "045": {"n":"Colour in the areas between lines","a":["Look in the drawing for areas completely enclosed by lines.","Colour in these areas.","Compare the coloured areas with places where there are only lines."],"m":"A line drawing of your own, coloured pencils","f":"What stands out more after colouring in?"},
  "046": {"n":"Build a line in space","a":["Draw a simple curved line.","Bend the pipe cleaner so that it resembles this line.","Now bend some parts upwards while others stay on the table."],"m":"Paper, pen, pipe cleaner","f":"How does the line change when you look at it from the side?"},
  "047": {"n":"Form a space from one sheet","a":["Fold the sheet and stand it up so that an opening appears underneath.","Change the shape with further folds or cuts.","Try to create several small openings or gaps with the same sheet."],"m":"A sheet of paper, scissors","f":"Which fold keeps your shape open?"},
  "048": {"n":"Make paper look like bark","a":["Look at the grooves and cracks of the bark.","Crumple, fold or tear your paper so that its surface becomes more like the bark.","Lay the result next to the original."],"m":"Paper, a piece of tree bark or a photo of it","f":"Which change makes the paper look most like bark?"},
  "049": {"n":"Make light paper look heavy","a":["Take a light piece of paper and look at it briefly.","Fold, roll or crumple it so that it looks as solid and heavy as possible.","Put it on the table and look at it from a little distance."],"m":"Thin paper","f":"What makes the shape look heavy although the material is light?"},
  "050": {"n":"Cut up and lay out anew","a":["Cut the drawing into at least eight pieces.","Put them together anew on the base. Turn and shift individual pieces.","Choose an arrangement you want to leave as it is."],"m":"A drawing of your own you can spare, scissors, a sheet as a base","f":"What of the original picture do you still recognise?"},
  "051": {"n":"Let paper shapes fall","a":["Let the shapes fall onto the base from a low height.","Look at how they lie without moving anything at once.","Then change the position of exactly three shapes."],"m":"Small paper shapes, a sheet as a base","f":"Which of your three changes makes the biggest difference?"},
  "052": {"n":"Continue a quick line","a":["Without a sketch, draw a quick line across the sheet.","Then look for two spots that interest you, such as a crossing or a loop.","Add further lines or areas only at these two spots."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"What do you recognise there after drawing on?"},
  "053": {"n":"Begin without looking","a":["Draw the object for one minute without looking at the paper.","Look at the result.","Add exactly three lines or areas. Leave the rest as it is."],"m":"A small object, paper, pen, timer","f":"Which unplanned spot do you want to leave unchanged?"},
  "054": {"n":"Compare print and original","a":["Paint a simple shape with one clearly longer side onto the plate.","Press a sheet onto the still wet paint and peel it off carefully.","Compare print and original. Add a new shape to the print."],"m":"Small plastic plate without sharp edges, gouache, brush, paper","f":"What has changed in the print about the direction?"},
  "055": {"n":"Reorder rubbed patterns","a":["Lay paper on each surface and rub over it with the flat pencil lead.","Cut a few shapes from the three patterns.","Put them together on a new sheet. You do not have to depict an object with them."],"m":"Paper, soft pencil, 3 firm surfaces, scissors","f":"Which pattern do you recognise even in its new shape?"},
  "056": {"n":"Draw, glue and build a subject","a":["Choose a simple subject, such as a tree.","First draw it on paper.","Then put it together from pieces of paper and finally build a small shape from modelling clay."],"m":"Paper, pen, scissors, glue, small pieces of paper, modelling clay","f":"How do you recognise the subject in all three versions?"},
  "057": {"n":"Lay two drawings over each other","a":["Draw a simple pattern on each sheet, for example circles and stripes.","Lay the sheets on top of each other.","Shift and turn the upper sheet. Look for a position you like."],"m":"2 sheets of tracing paper, pen","f":"Which shape only appears through the overlay?"},
  "058": {"n":"Distort a picture with boxes","a":["Draw a grid of three by three equally sized boxes. Draw a simple subject into it.","Draw a second grid next to it whose middle column is twice as wide.","Transfer the subject box by box into the new grid."],"m":"Paper, pencil, ruler","f":"Which part of the subject changes the most?"},
  "059": {"n":"A shape changes","a":["Divide the sheet into three fields.","Draw the same shape three times: before a change, during it and after it. Show, for example, a circle slowly opening.","Leave out explanatory words."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"How do you recognise the order of the three pictures?"},
  "060": {"n":"Begin with a leftover","a":["Choose a leftover whose shape or colour interests you.","Place it on a new sheet and glue it down.","Add lines or shapes that connect to it."],"m":"A paper leftover from an earlier work, paper, pen, glue","f":"Which quality of the leftover guided you as you worked on?"},
  "061": {"n":"A picture with three colours","a":["Choose three coloured pencils.","Draw and colour a small picture of simple shapes with them.","Keep the colours separate so that no further mixed colours appear. White paper may remain visible."],"m":"Paper, 3 coloured pencils","f":"Which of the three colours do you notice first?"},
  "062": {"n":"Repeat a shape many times","a":["Choose a basic shape, for example a triangle.","Draw it several times on the sheet: small and large, turned and partly overlapping.","Use only this one kind of shape."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"Which change brings the most variety into the picture for you?"},
  "063": {"n":"A picture from lines","a":["On one part of the sheet draw lines densely side by side.","In another place set a few lines with large gaps.","Add lines in another direction until a picture appears for you."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"Where do the lines work together like a surface?"},
  "064": {"n":"Regular or free shapes","a":["Choose a rule: only circles, triangles and rectangles, or only freely drawn shapes with irregular edges.","Fill part of the sheet with shapes from your chosen group.","Keep the rule until the end."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"Which shape would you have liked to add that your rule does not allow?"},
  "065": {"n":"Open or closed shapes","a":["Decide on open outlines with a gap or on completely closed outlines.","Draw a small picture with them.","At the end check whether all shapes follow your rule."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"Which shape looks particularly open or particularly closed to you?"},
  "066": {"n":"Mirror image with one deviation","a":["Fold the sheet in the middle and open it again.","Draw shapes as mirror-like as possible to the left and right of the fold line.","On one side change exactly one small spot."],"m":"Paper, pencil, coloured pencil","f":"How quickly do you find the changed spot when you look again?"},
  "067": {"n":"Balance three shapes","a":["Place the three shapes on the base, such as a large circle, a small triangle and a narrow strip.","Shift them until no side feels too full or too empty to you.","Avoid a mirror-symmetrical arrangement."],"m":"3 different paper shapes, a sheet as a base","f":"Which shape did you have to move the most to achieve that?"},
  "068": {"n":"Leave the middle free","a":["Mark the middle of the sheet very lightly with a small dot.","Draw a picture of shapes without touching the dot.","Look at the picture from a little distance."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"Where do you look first, although the middle of the sheet is not there?"},
  "069": {"n":"One shape should stand out","a":["Draw a shape that should stand out first later on.","Add further shapes. Choose their size, colour and position so that the first shape stays striking.","Look away briefly and then at the sheet again."],"m":"Paper, coloured pencils","f":"What determines which shape you notice first?"},
  "070": {"n":"Which area becomes the shape","a":["Draw an irregular line from the top to the bottom edge of the sheet.","Paint the area to its left black. Leave the right area white.","First look at the black side as the shape and the white as the background. Then try it the other way round."],"m":"White paper, black felt pen","f":"Can you switch between the two ways of seeing?"},
  "071": {"n":"Half stays empty","a":["Imagine a line dividing the sheet into two equal halves.","Design a picture only on one side of this line.","Leave the other half completely empty."],"m":"Paper, coloured pencils","f":"How does the empty half affect the picture?"},
  "072": {"n":"From dense to loose","a":["On the left of the sheet set many small dots densely side by side.","Work on towards the right and gradually increase the gaps.","At the right edge leave only a few single dots."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"Where do you still recognise single dots, where rather a surface?"},
  "073": {"n":"Draw a recurring sequence","a":["Choose a shape, for example a circle.","Draw it in the sequence small, small, large. Repeat this group of three at least four times.","Leave a little more space between the groups."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"Can you recognise the recurring groups at a glance?"},
  "074": {"n":"Change a rule slowly","a":["Draw six equally sized shapes with equal gaps in a row.","Continue the row. Now change the gaps little by little.","Let the last shapes be clearly more irregularly spaced."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"From which point do you notice the change?"},
  "075": {"n":"A shape in three sizes","a":["Choose a shape and draw it once small, once medium and once large.","Fill part of the sheet with repetitions of these three templates.","Use no other sizes."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"Which of the three sizes do you notice first in the finished picture?"},
  "076": {"n":"Squares with fixed sizes","a":["Draw squares with sides of 1, 2 and 4 centimetres and cut them out.","Place the three squares on the base.","Shift them until you like the distribution."],"m":"Paper, ruler, pen, scissors, a sheet as a base","f":"Which distance between the squares feels right to you?"},
  "077": {"n":"Only paper and string","a":["Build a small shape from these two materials only.","Fold or roll the paper and join it with the string, for example by wrapping and knotting.","Leave both materials visible."],"m":"Paper, string","f":"What does the string make possible that the paper alone could not?"},
  "078": {"n":"Build without glue","a":["Cut at least three parts from the cardboard.","Join them by folding, clamping or slotting into each other.","Build a small shape from them that stands by itself. Use no glue."],"m":"Cardboard leftovers, scissors","f":"Which joint holds most reliably?"},
  "079": {"n":"Hold parts together by weight","a":["Build a small bridge or tower from the parts.","Stack, lean or wedge the parts so that they hold together without glue or string.","Move one part carefully and watch when the shape becomes more or less stable."],"m":"Light building blocks and pieces of cardboard","f":"Which part holds something else in place by its weight?"},
  "080": {"n":"Show an idea with paper","a":["Choose an idea, for example «rising», «fanning out» or «getting denser».","Fold, tear or roll pieces of paper so that you can show the idea with them.","Arrange the pieces into a small composition."],"m":"A few sheets of paper","f":"Where is your chosen idea easiest to recognise?"},
  "081": {"n":"Exactly ten cuts","a":["Cut the paper with exactly ten cuts. Each cut runs from one edge to another.","Count the cuts and stop after the tenth.","Put the resulting pieces together into a picture on the base."],"m":"Paper, scissors, a sheet as a base","f":"Which piece determines your arrangement the most?"},
  "082": {"n":"Only curved lines","a":["Draw a picture in which every line is curved.","Try tight and wide arcs as well as small and large curves.","Connect some lines and let others stand alone."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"What makes your picture look ordered or disordered?"},
  "083": {"n":"Only straight lines","a":["Draw a picture only from straight lines.","Let the lines run in different directions and meet in some places.","Add narrow and wide angles."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"Where does movement seem to arise for you?"},
  "084": {"n":"One line through the whole sheet","a":["Put the pen down at one spot on the sheet.","Lead a single line through different areas of the sheet. It may cross itself.","Lift the pen only when you want to end the drawing."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"Where does your line create closed shapes?"},
  "085": {"n":"Draw space with black and white","a":["Draw several shapes that partly cover each other.","Paint some black and leave others white.","Use no grey hatching or further colours."],"m":"White paper, black felt pen","f":"Which shape seems to lie in front, which behind?"},
  "086": {"n":"A picture with five greys","a":["Draw five small boxes at the edge.","Leave the first white. Hatch the others progressively darker until the last is very dark.","Draw simple shapes underneath and fill them with these five tones."],"m":"Paper, soft pencil","f":"Which shapes stand out clearly from each other?"},
  "087": {"n":"Two opposite colours","a":["Paint a few red and green areas on the sheet.","Let some areas border directly on each other and others be separated by white paper.","Use only these two pencils."],"m":"White paper, red and green coloured pencil","f":"Where do the two colours strike you most strongly?"},
  "088": {"n":"Similar colours in the picture","a":["Choose similar colours, for example light green, dark green and blue-green.","Paint large and small areas with them, some in the middle and others at the edge.","Look at the picture from a little distance."],"m":"Paper, 3 to 5 coloured pencils in similar shades","f":"Which area seems nearer, which further away, if you see a difference?"},
  "089": {"n":"Same colours in other amounts","a":["On the first sheet paint four areas of colour: one large, two medium and one very small.","On the second sheet paint the same colours in similar places.","This time swap the sizes of the largest and the smallest area."],"m":"2 sheets, 4 coloured pencils","f":"Which colour do you notice first on each sheet?"},
  "090": {"n":"Stop by a fixed rule","a":["Before you begin, decide that your drawing will consist of exactly 20 strokes.","Draw and count each stroke.","Put the pen down after the twentieth stroke."],"m":"Paper, pen","f":"What would you still add if the rule allowed it?"},
  "091": {"n":"Draw without looking at the sheet","a":["Put the object in front of you and set the pen on the paper.","Follow its outer edge slowly with your eyes. Move the pen along at the same time without lifting it.","Look at the drawing only once you have gone all the way round the object."],"m":"A small object, paper, pen","f":"Which part of the object do you recognise in your drawing?"},
  "092": {"n":"Draw an outline slowly","a":["Choose a starting point on the edge of the object.","Trace the outline slowly. Let eyes and hand travel at the same speed as far as possible.","Look alternately at the object and at the paper."],"m":"A small object, paper, pen","f":"Where did you have to look particularly closely?"},
  "093": {"n":"Draw in 30 seconds","a":["Set the timer to 30 seconds.","Draw only the most important directions and the rough shape of your model.","Repeat four more times in free spots on the sheet."],"m":"An object or a photo of a movement, paper, pen, timer","f":"Which lines reappear in several drawings?"},
  "094": {"n":"Draw with the other hand","a":["Draw the object with your usual writing hand.","Draw it again next to it with the other hand.","Compare the two drawings."],"m":"A small object, paper, pen","f":"What differences do you see in the lines?"},
  "095": {"n":"Five minutes without lifting the pen","a":["Choose a simple subject, for example a plant.","Set the timer to five minutes and draw without lifting the pen.","If you want to continue somewhere else, connect the places with a line."],"m":"Paper, pen, timer","f":"Which connections came about because of the rule?"},
  "096": {"n":"Draw with the whole arm","a":["Position the paper so that your arm has plenty of room.","Draw large arcs and long lines by moving the whole arm from the shoulder.","Try several directions."],"m":"Large paper, soft pencil or chalk","f":"Which lines differ from your usual small hand movements?"},
  "097": {"n":"Draw lightly and firmly","a":["Draw ten lines of roughly equal length one below the other.","Press only lightly for the first line. Increase the pressure a little with each further line.","Keep the same grip on the pen as far as possible."],"m":"Paper, pencil","f":"Between which lines is the difference clearest?"},
  "098": {"n":"Five marks with one brush","a":["Paint one mark with the tip of the brush and one with its broad side.","Try one mark each with little and with plenty of water.","Turn the brush while painting for a fifth mark."],"m":"Paper, brush, watercolour, water","f":"Which mark would you like to use again?"},
  "099": {"n":"Paint with other tools","a":["Pick up some paint with the plastic card and drag it across the paper.","Dab paint next to it with the sponge.","Draw another mark with the wooden stick. Compare the three results."],"m":"Paper, liquid poster paint, old plastic card, sponge, wooden stick","f":"Which mark could you predict the least?"}
};
